mock_essays=[
('Accessible materials','Should accessibility checks be compulsory before course material is published?', '''Universities should require an accessibility check before course materials are published. Learning resources cannot fulfil their purpose when some students are unable to read or navigate them. A routine check would make inclusion part of ordinary preparation rather than an emergency response after complaints.

First, accessible documents allow more students to study independently. A clearly structured text file can support searching, adjustable display settings and screen-reading software. By contrast, an image-only scan may contain the correct information while preventing effective use. Checking these features before distribution reduces avoidable barriers without changing the academic standard of the course.

Second, an organised process can save repeated remedial work. If every teacher receives a short checklist and an appropriate document template, common problems can be identified early. The library or support team can then focus on difficult cases. This is more efficient than asking individual students to repeatedly explain the same obstacle after each upload.

Critics may argue that compulsory checking adds to an already demanding workload. That concern supports proportionate procedures and staff training, rather than leaving access to chance. A brief initial check can cover searchable text, useful headings and readable alternatives for essential images. Specialist help should remain available where a simple check is insufficient.

A requirement alone will not guarantee perfect access. Universities must collect feedback and correct problems that appear in use. Nevertheless, publication checks establish clear responsibility and reduce predictable failures. They recognise that a resource is ready only when students can use it to engage with the ideas it contains effectively and independently.'''),
('Repair culture','Should workplaces prioritise safe repair over immediate equipment replacement?', '''Workplaces should prioritise safe repair before replacing faulty equipment. This policy can reduce avoidable disposal and improve purchasing decisions, provided that safety and reliability remain essential conditions. Repair should be a reasoned assessment rather than a commitment to keep every old device indefinitely.

First, inspection prevents usable equipment from being discarded because of a minor fault. A damaged connector or replaceable component does not necessarily make an entire device worthless. Qualified technicians can assess whether restoration is practical and test the result before returning it to service. This process gives the organisation a clearer basis for choosing between repair and replacement.

Second, repair records reveal recurring weaknesses. If several devices fail in the same way, the purchasing team can consider durability and maintenance support when selecting future equipment. Recording both the cost and success of repairs also prevents a vague environmental promise from concealing expensive or unreliable practices. Decisions become easier to review and explain.

Opponents reasonably worry that repairing equipment may interrupt work or create safety risks. Those risks justify clear limits. Unsafe devices should be withdrawn immediately, and temporary replacements can support essential tasks during inspection. Where repeated repairs cost more than a dependable replacement, the organisation should replace the item rather than defend repair at any price.

The strongest policy therefore combines technical judgement with transparent records. Managers should assess service reliability alongside waste reduction and cost. Safe repair deserves priority because it treats replacement as a decision requiring evidence, while preserving the organisation's responsibility to provide equipment that staff can depend on throughout their normal working day.'''),
('Community participation','Should colleges recognise community volunteering as part of student development?', '''Colleges should recognise supervised community volunteering as a valuable part of student development. Participation can connect academic learning with practical responsibility, but recognition should respect community needs and students' different circumstances. A voluntary, well-supported programme offers more value than a compulsory competition for service hours.

First, community work can develop skills that lectures alone cannot fully exercise. A student helping at a reading centre must listen, explain ideas clearly and adapt to different learners. These experiences create opportunities to practise patience and teamwork. Reflection afterwards can connect the activity with broader questions about communication, inclusion and responsible participation in shared institutions.

Second, a college can provide continuity that isolated volunteering often lacks. Staff can establish a partnership with a community organisation, train participants and agree on realistic duties. This makes the programme more dependable for the host and gives students a clear understanding of their responsibilities. Community feedback should help determine whether the activity remains useful.

Critics may argue that recognition turns service into a credential and disadvantages students with limited free time. This concern is justified when institutions reward hours without considering circumstances. Colleges should therefore offer flexible opportunities and recognise thoughtful participation rather than impose a universal quota. Students who cannot attend should retain access to other developmental activities.

Volunteering should also complement trained staff rather than replace necessary employment. With supervision, flexible participation and honest evaluation, colleges can support a meaningful relationship between learning and service. Recognition is worthwhile when it encourages sustained responsibility and respects the people whom the programme is intended to support.''')]
mock_short=[
('Equipment loan','The library introduced a short-term laptop loan scheme for students whose own devices were being repaired. Borrowers received a checklist explaining return dates and responsibility for damage. Staff checked each laptop before and after use. The scheme was reviewed after one term using waiting-list records and student feedback. The library intended to address temporary access problems rather than replace the need for a wider equipment-support policy.', 'Students temporarily without a computer because of repairs could borrow one from a new library service. A written guide set out when equipment had to be returned and the borrower’s obligations if it was damaged. Employees inspected devices on collection and return. After a term, feedback and waiting lists informed a review. The initiative addressed short interruptions in access while acknowledging that broader equipment support was still necessary.'),
('Quiet hours','A residence introduced quiet hours after consulting its occupants. The rule applied to shared corridors as well as individual rooms. Residents could report persistent disruption to a duty officer rather than confront others directly. After four weeks, the residence committee reviewed complaints and invited suggestions. It planned to revise the arrangement if it created unexpected difficulties, while retaining the aim of protecting time for rest and study.', 'Following discussion with residents, a hostel established periods when noise had to be limited in both rooms and common passageways. A duty officer could receive reports of repeated disturbance, avoiding the need for direct personal disputes. Four weeks later, the committee examined complaints and sought proposals. The policy remained open to adjustment if unforeseen problems arose, but its central purpose was still to safeguard opportunities to study and rest.'),
('Shared garden','A neighbourhood group created a shared garden on a small unused plot. Members agreed to rotate watering duties and keep a record of expenses. The group chose plants suited to local conditions instead of attempting to grow a wide range immediately. Visitors could suggest improvements through a monthly meeting. The first season was treated as a trial, allowing the group to learn about maintenance before expanding the project.', 'An unused piece of land became a communal garden managed by local residents. They shared watering responsibilities through a rota and documented spending. Rather than begin with many varieties, participants selected plants appropriate to the area. Monthly meetings provided a way for visitors to propose changes. Before considering a larger garden, the group used the initial season to test the arrangements and understand the care the site required.')]
mock_email=[
('Request a room','Write to the library manager requesting a room for six students to discuss a group project next Tuesday, 3–4 p.m. Mention noise control and ask for booking confirmation.', '''Subject: Group study room request for Tuesday, 3–4 p.m.

Dear Library Manager,

I am writing to request a group study room for six students next Tuesday from 3 to 4 p.m. We need to discuss the findings of our course project before preparing a joint presentation.

We will bring our own laptops and require only seating and a working power point. We understand that discussion should take place in a designated room rather than in the quiet reading area. We will keep the door closed, avoid amplified sound and leave the space ready for the next users.

Could you please confirm whether a suitable room is available and explain the booking procedure? If that time is unavailable, an alternative between 2 and 5 p.m. would also be helpful.

Thank you for considering our request.

Kind regards,
Aditi Bose
Student Project Coordinator'''),
('Complaint about duplicate charge','Write to an online training provider: you paid a workshop fee once, but your statement shows two charges. Request investigation and refund of the duplicate.', '''Subject: Duplicate workshop fee charge, registration WR-512

Dear Accounts Team,

I am writing about a duplicate charge for workshop registration WR-512. I authorised one payment of Rs 1,200 on 6 November, but my statement shows two charges of the same amount under your organisation’s name.

I received one registration confirmation and intended to reserve only one place. Copies of the payment receipt and the relevant statement entries are available for verification. Please let me know the appropriate channel for sharing them securely.

Could you investigate the second charge and refund it if it is confirmed as a duplicate? I would appreciate acknowledgement of this request and an indication of the expected review time. Please also confirm that my original workshop registration will remain valid.

Thank you for your assistance. I look forward to your update.

Yours sincerely,
Kabir Jain'''),
('Enquiry about volunteering','Write to a community centre enquiring about weekend volunteering. Ask about duties, training, timings and how to apply.', '''Subject: Enquiry about weekend volunteering opportunities

Dear Community Centre Coordinator,

I am a college student interested in supporting your weekend activities. I am writing to ask whether you currently accept volunteers and what responsibilities a new participant could undertake.

I can usually attend on Saturday afternoons and would be particularly interested in supervised reading activities. Could you please share the session timings, minimum commitment and any eligibility requirements? I would also appreciate information about introductory training and the staff supervision provided during sessions.

Please let me know how to apply and whether you require references or an initial meeting. I would be happy to discuss my availability before making a commitment, so that the arrangement meets the centre’s needs.

Thank you for your time and guidance. I look forward to learning more about the programme.

Kind regards,
Leela Nair''')]
