'Laptop loan requests rose overall between August and November, although the final month recorded a decline. Requests increased from 30 in August to 50 in November, a net gain of 20, or approximately 66.7%. They rose to 45 in September and reached a peak of 60 in October. Each of these first two monthly increases was 15 requests. November then recorded 10 fewer requests than October. August had the lowest total, while the difference between the highest and lowest monthly values was 30. The November figure remained above both the August and September figures despite the final decline. These are counts of requests rather than unique borrowers or completed loans. The chart therefore establishes demand recorded by month without showing whether every request resulted in a loan.',
'The Software workshop had the largest number of attendees, but Writing and Software shared the highest attendance rate. Writing attracted 30 registrations and 24 attendees, giving a rate of 80%. Safety recorded 40 registrations and 20 attendees, equivalent to 50%. Software had 50 registrations and 40 attendees, also an 80% rate. Thus, Software exceeded Writing by 16 attendees while matching its proportion attending. Safety had the lowest attendance rate, 30 percentage points below the other two workshops, and 20 of its registered participants did not attend. Across all three events, 84 of 120 registered people attended, producing an overall rate of 70%. This combined rate uses the total counts rather than the simple average of the three percentages. The chart does not explain why registered participants missed a session.',
